WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.517
This slide collects several mistakes that are easy to make when learning SQL.

00:00:04.517 --> 00:00:08.032
Use IS NULL rather than equality when testing missing values.

00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:12.397
When AND and OR appear together, add parentheses so the intended logic is visible.

00:00:12.397 --> 00:00:17.527
Do not use LIMIT to claim you have the top or latest rows unless ORDER BY defines what top or latest

00:00:17.527 --> 00:00:23.462
means. Avoid updating records by a name when a stable unique key is available.

00:00:23.462 --> 00:00:26.282
And before DELETE, preview the target rows with SELECT.

00:00:26.282 --> 00:00:30.497
These habits may look small, but they prevent large errors.

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:33.857
When debugging, do not change many parts of the query at once.

00:00:33.857 --> 00:00:38.597
Read the requirement, identify the specific clause that is responsible, change one thing, run it again,

00:00:38.597 --> 00:00:39.852
and inspect what changed.

00:00:39.852 --> 00:00:44.797
Connect this idea to an analyst exploring operational records before building a report.

00:00:44.797 --> 00:00:49.697
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:49.697 --> 00:00:54.522
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:54.522 --> 00:00:59.697
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:59.697 --> 00:01:04.429
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
